# Docker build context exclusions.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: without it the context is ~549M, of which
# `backend/typescript/node_modules` alone is 453M — and the Dockerfile never
# copies it (it copies package.json + package-lock.json and runs `npm ci`
# itself, twice: once in the builder stage with dev deps, once in the runtime
# stage with --omit=dev). Uploading it to the daemon is pure waste.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# READ BEFORE ADDING AN EXCLUSION — the trap this file is built around.
#
# `build-meta/commit.txt` is GITIGNORED (deliberately: scripts/preflight-deploy.sh
# refuses a dirty tree, and this file is regenerated every deploy). It is ALSO
# load-bearing: the Dockerfile does `COPY build-meta ./build-meta`, and that file
# is what makes /api/readiness report `build.commit`. ADR 0518 + scripts/
# verify-deploy.sh treat `unknown` as a FAILED deploy, because an unstamped
# image is indistinguishable from a stale one.
#
# So DO NOT model this file on .gitignore. The sibling `.gcloudignore` does
# (`#!include:.gitignore`) and therefore needs an explicit `!build-meta/commit.txt`
# negation — which was caught pre-merge on 2026-08-10 only by diffing
# `gcloud meta list-files-for-upload`. That failure is SILENT and test-invisible:
# the unit tests point at a temp dir via OPENWOP_BUILD_META_DIR, so they pass
# whether or not the real file ever ships.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-13, both sabotages, because the two failure modes differ and
# only one of them is dangerous:
#   exclude `build-meta`            → docker build FAILS (rc=1). Loud. Harmless.
#   exclude `build-meta/commit.txt` → docker build SUCCEEDS (rc=0), and the image
#                                     reports commit: unknown.
# The second is the one a .gitignore-derived list produces, because .gitignore
# ignores `commit.txt` but NOT the tracked `.gitkeep` beside it — so the
# directory still ships and `COPY build-meta` is satisfied by an empty dir.
# A green build that produced an unidentifiable artifact is the exact shape of
# gate this repo keeps finding; see docs/adr/0550 P2.
#
# This file avoids the trap structurally instead: it is an ALLOW-BY-DEFAULT list
# that excludes only build products and dependency trees, and never mentions any
# path the Dockerfile COPYs. Every COPY source below must stay un-excluded:
#   backend/typescript/{package.json,package-lock.json,tsconfig.json,
#                       vitest.config.ts,src,scripts,vendor}
#   providers.json  conformance-fixtures  schemas  packs  distributions
#   examples/workflow-chain-packs  examples/connection-packs  build-meta
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Dependency trees — reinstalled inside the image from the lockfile.
**/node_modules

# Version control. (Small in a git worktree, where .git is a file; large in the
# main checkout, which is the case that matters.)
.git
.gitignore

# Build products — the image builds its own.
backend/typescript/lib
frontend/react/dist
frontend/react/.vite

# Test/CI output.
coverage
playwright-report
test-results
**/*.log

# Local editor / OS noise.
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
.idea
.vscode

# Local env files — secrets must never enter a build context. No negation here:
# this Dockerfile builds the BACKEND only, and frontend/react/.env.production is
# consumed by `vite build` on the host, never inside the image.
.env
.env.*
